**Key responsibilities of a Cyber Security Operations Centre (C‑SOC) – RBI 2019, Annex IV, Level IV**  
(i) protect critical business and customer data/information and demonstrate compliance with internal guidelines, country regulations and laws;  
(ii) provide real‑time / near‑real‑time information on and insight into the security posture of the UCB;  
(iii) manage security operations by preparing for and responding to cyber‑risks/threats, and facilitate continuity and recovery;  
(iv) know who did what, when, how and preserve evidence;  
(v) integrate various log types and logging options into a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system, including ticketing/work‑flow/case management, reporting/dashboard, use‑case/rule design, and handling unstructured/big‑data;  
(vi) monitor network‑activity logs and have the capability to flag and escalat​e any abnormal or undesirable activity;  
(vii) monitor, analyse and escalat​e security incidents; develop response plans; conduct incident management and forensic analysis; and coordinate with relevant internal stakeholders and external agencies. [1]  

**First technological step in setting up a C‑SOC**  
The first step is “to arrive at a suitable and cost‑effective technology framework designed and implemented to ensure proactive monitoring capabilities aligned with the banking technology risk profile and business and regulatory requirements.” [1]

